# otto-factory — machine-level config
#
# This file lives inside the otto-factory folder and controls settings that
# apply to EVERY project on this machine: where to find the CLI tools, and
# global timeout and retry defaults.
#
# Project-specific settings (GITHUB_REPO, TEST_COMMAND, SOURCE_GLOB) go in a
# .otto file inside each project folder — see .otto.example for that template.
#
# HOW TO USE:
#   1. Copy this file to .env:  cp .env.example .env
#   2. Most people can leave .env exactly as-is — the defaults work for
#      standard installations. Only uncomment and change a line if you have
#      a specific reason to (described in the comments below).
#
# Lines starting with # are comments and are ignored. To activate a setting,
# remove the # at the start of that line and set the value after the =.


# ── Tool paths ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Otto-factory calls the `claude` and `gh` programs to do its work.
# By default it looks for them on your PATH (the standard way programs are
# found on a computer). You only need to change these if you get an error
# like "command not found: claude" even though you know it is installed.
#
# To find the correct path, run this in your terminal:
#   which claude     ← copy the result and paste it after CLAUDE_BIN=
#   which gh         ← copy the result and paste it after GH_BIN=
#
# GH_BIN=gh
# CLAUDE_BIN=claude


# ── Global defaults ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# How long (in seconds) to wait for the coder to finish one issue.
# Default is 600 seconds (10 minutes). Increase this for very large or
# complex tasks that take longer to complete.
# CODER_TIMEOUT=600

# How many times to retry a failing issue before giving up and flagging it
# for your attention. Default is 3 attempts.
# MAX_ATTEMPTS=3

# Shell initialisation command prepended to TEST_COMMAND before running tests.
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS: Otto-factory runs TEST_COMMAND in a non-interactive shell
# that does not load your normal startup files (~/.bashrc, ~/.zshrc). If your
# test runner (node, python, ruby, etc.) is installed via a version manager
# like nvm, pyenv, or asdf, it won't be on PATH unless you initialise it here.
#
# HOW TO KNOW IF YOU NEED THIS:
#   1. Run `which npm` (or `which python`, `which node`, etc.) in your terminal.
#   2. If the path shown contains ".nvm", ".asdf", ".pyenv", or similar, you
#      need SHELL_INIT.
#   3. Look in your ~/.bashrc or ~/.zshrc for the line that loads your version
#      manager and copy it here.
#
# If `which npm` returns a plain path like /usr/local/bin/npm, you do not
# need this setting — leave it commented out.
#
# Common examples:
#   SHELL_INIT=source ~/.nvm/nvm.sh && nvm use node --silent   # nvm (Node.js)
#   SHELL_INIT=source ~/.asdf/asdf.sh                          # asdf (multi-language)
#   SHELL_INIT=source ~/.rvm/scripts/rvm                       # rvm (Ruby)
#
# SHELL_INIT=
